solution_md:
The shortest possible circumradius is when point  $P$  is arbitrarily close to  $Q$  and  $RQ$  is perpendicular to  $AB$ . This gives a lowest bound of  $\tfrac12$  exclusive.
[asy]
unitsize(50);
draw((0, 0)--(0, 1)--(1, 1)--(1, 0)--cycle);
draw((0.5, 1)--(0.5, 0)--(0.48, 1));
draw(circumcircle((0.5, 1), (0.5, 0), (0.48, 1)), dotted);
dot(circumcenter((0.5, 1), (0.5, 0), (0.48, 1)));
label(" $O$ ", circumcenter((0.5, 1), (0.5, 0), (0.4, 1)), W);
label(" $A$ ", (0, 1), NW);
label(" $B$ ", (1, 1), NE);
label(" $C$ ", (1, 0), SE);
label(" $D$ ", (0, 0), SW);
label(" $P$ ", (0.48, 1), NW);
label(" $Q$ ", (0.5, 1), NE);
label(" $R$ ", (0.5, 0), S);
[/asy]

The largest possible circumradius is created when P is diametrically opposed to  $R$  and  $RQ$  is perpendicular to  $AB$ . The longest length of this triangle is  $\sqrt2$  by the Pythagorean Theorem which gives the upper bound  $\dfrac{\sqrt2}2$  inclusive.
[asy]
unitsize(50);
draw((0, 0)--(0, 1)--(1, 1)--(1, 0)--cycle);
draw((0, 1)--(1, 0)--(1, 1));
draw(circumcircle((1, 1), (1, 0), (0, 1)), dotted);
dot(circumcenter((1, 1), (1, 0), (0, 1)));
label(" $O$ ", circumcenter((1, 1), (1, 0), (0, 1)), W);
label(" $A$ ", (0, 1), NW);
label(" $B$ ", (1, 1), NE);
label(" $C$ ", (1, 0), SE);
label(" $D$ ", (0, 0), SW);
label(" $P$ ", (0, 1), NE);
label(" $Q$ ", (1, 1), NW);
label(" $R$ ", (1, 0), SW);
[/asy]

Thus, the range of values is  $R\in\boxed{\left(\dfrac12, \dfrac{\sqrt2}2\right]}$ .

