solution_md:
WLOG,  $P(x)$  has the roots  $x = 0,\pm k$  for any real  $k \neq 0$ .  This gives us: $P(x) = x(x - k)(x + k) = x^3 - (k^2)x$ ; $P'(x) = 3x^2 - k^2$ ; $P''(x) = 6x$ .

The roots of  $[P'(x)]^2 - 2P(x)*P''(x) = 0$  are computed according to: $(3x^2 - k^2)^2 - 2(x^3 - (k^2)x)(6x) = 0$ ;

or  $9x^4 - 6k^2x^2 + k^4 - 12x^4 + 12k^2x^2 = 0$ ;

or  $-3x^4 + 6k^2x^2 + k^4 = 0$ ;

or  $-3x^4 + 6k^2x^2 -3k^4 + 4k^4 = 0$ ;

or  $4k^4 = 3(x^2 - k^2)^2$ ;

or  $x^2 = k^2 \pm (2k^2)/\sqrt{3}$ ;

or  $x = \pm k\sqrt{1 \pm 2/\sqrt{3}}$ .

The quantity  $1 - 2/\sqrt{3}$  is negative and is eliminated since we require  $x$  to be real-valued.  Hence, there are two real roots:  $x = \pm k\sqrt{1 + 2/\sqrt{3}}$ .

