What a troll problem.

Let  $\theta_k=\arctan{a_k} \in (-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2} )$ , the condition implies that for non-negative integer  $n$ ,
\[ a_n=i \cdot \frac{P_n(i)-\overline{P_n(i)}}{P_n(i)+\overline{P_n(i)}}=i \cdot \frac{2i \cdot \text{Im} P_n(i)}{2 \cdot \text{Re} P_n(i)}=-\frac{\text{Im} P_n(i)}{\text{Re} P_n(i)}. \]
Hence,
\begin{align*}
\theta_n&=\arctan(a_n)=-\text{arg}(P_n(i))=-\text{arg} \left( \prod_{j=-k}^{n-1} (1-a_j i) \right) 
&\equiv -\sum_{j=-k}^{n-1} \text{arg} (1-a_j i) \pmod{\pi} 
&\equiv \sum_{j=-k}^{n-1} \theta_j \pmod{\pi}
\end{align*}
For non-negative integer  $n$  we have  $\theta_{n+1}-\theta_n \equiv \theta_n \pmod{\pi}$ , i.e.  $\theta_{n+1} \equiv 2\theta_n \pmod{\pi}$ , so  $\theta_n=2^n \theta_0 \pmod{\pi}$ . The process stops only if  $\theta_n = \frac{\pi}{2}$ .
If  $a_m=a_n$ , then  $\theta_m \equiv \theta_n \pmod{\pi}$ , hence  $\theta_k$  is eventually periodic, therefore the process never ends.

